  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Confidence is high for organized strong to\nsevere thunderstorms arriving late Monday night, and \nredeveloping across the area on Tuesday.\n\nA series of short waves moving south in NNW flow aloft will \ntrigger showers and storms tonight. At the surface, a lee trough\nsituated in the western Piedmont will help focus cells. With \nwarming this afternoon, there should be enough instability to \nsupport an occasional stronger storm with damaging winds, but \noverall the severe threat looks marginal. After about midnight, \nconvection exits to the east, followed by another warm and damp \nnight with stratus and fog. Monday looks mostly rain-free with \ndrier air in the region, although the lingering surface trough \nin the area may bring isolated showers and a storm or two to the\neastern Piedmont. \n\nOn Monday night, a warm front will lift across the region into \nTuesday morning. At the same time, a strong short wave will \napproach from the NW. These features will support widespread \nrain with embedded storms, a few which may be strong to severe \nwith damaging winds, in the form of a bowing MCS traveling from \nthe OH Valley. CAMs are now steering the brunt of this feature \nto our west, over central and eastern KY. If so, we may just get\naway with some heavy rain and less of a severe threat, at least\nearly on. As the day progresses, a surface low sags south \ntowards the region, and the short wave train continues. Severe \nweather in the afternoon will depend on how long the morning \nconvection lasts and how much time the atmosphere has to \nrecover, or destabilize again, ahead of the front/upper level \nwave. It appears we should have at least a few hours with breaks\nin cloud cover before the front arrives in the afternoon. \n\nSevere thunderstorms with wet microburst potential look likely \nas long as we can destabilize. Consistent severe weather\nforecasts by several models support this scenario. The ECMWF \nEFI and Shift of Tails for CAPE/shear has a bullseye over much \nof SW VA and NW NC Tuesday. Deep layer shear looks to peak \naround 40 kts, and current timing of the boundaries and diving \nshort wave energy/jet streak is around peak heating. We continue\nto be outlooked on Tuesday for a Slight Risk of severe weather \nfrom SPC, and a Marginal Risk of excessive rainfall from WPC. \nDamaging winds and heavy rain possibly leading to flash flooding\nwill be the main concerns. \n\nThe front and trough axis move towards the coast and offshore by \nWednesday morning. Upslope flow may bring a few showers to the \nmountains, but the severe threat will be over. Thursday and \nFriday also look quiet, with a return to scattered afternoon \nshowers and storms Saturday.",
